---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GuideRelated from '../../../components/GuideRelated.astro';
import { SITE_EN } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'Convert AI Illustrations to Editable SVG Locally (0 VRAM) — AIGC Studio';
const description =
  'No cloud uploads, no per-image fees, and zero VRAM required. Use AIGC Studio’s built-in WASM VTracer to turn flat illustrations, icons, and badges into clean editable .svg files.';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    inLanguage: 'en',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE_EN.name },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE_EN.name },
    mainEntityOfPage: `${SITE_EN.domain}/en/guides/ai-image-to-svg`,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: [
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Does converting images to SVG in AIGC Studio cost credits or require internet?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'No. Vectorization runs offline inside the local Node host via @visioncortex/vtracer WASM—zero VRAM, zero uploads, and unlimited conversions.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Should I vectorize photorealistic photos to SVG?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'No. Vector paths excel at crisp flat illustrations, logos, badges, UI icons, and poster graphics. Tracing high-frequency photographs creates tens of thousands of tiny shapes larger than a PNG.',
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/guides/ai-image-to-svg" lang="en" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="section section-first">
    <div class="container article-wrap">
      <div class="section-idx">GUIDE / VECTOR</div>
      <h1>Convert AI Illustrations to Editable SVG Locally</h1>
      <div class="article-meta">
        <span>Audience: Graphic &amp; UI designers</span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>4 min read</span>
      </div>

      <p>
        AI generators produce icons, badges, flat posters, and brand marks quickly—but production handoff demands crisp infinite scaling, easy recoloring, and editable shapes. Most online vectorizers charge monthly subscriptions and upload client drafts to external servers.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Offline zero-VRAM architecture:</strong>
        AIGC Studio’s “Vectorize to SVG” embeds <code>@visioncortex/vtracer</code> (Node WASM) to trace color regions and curves locally. It burns zero cloud GPU minutes, needs no Python setup, and never leaves your machine.
      </div>

      <h2>1. Measured Output Comparison</h2>
      <p>
        Left: jagged upscaled edges from a 32×32 bitmap. Right: an actual <code>.svg</code> vector file fitted locally by VTracer—smooth at every zoom level:
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap" style="margin: 26px 0 32px; padding: 20px;">
        <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 18px; align-items: center;">
          <figure style="margin: 0; text-align: center;">
            <div class="ws-checker" style="padding: 20px; border: 1px solid var(--line);">
              <img src="/assets/workshop/vector-before.webp" alt="Low-resolution input bitmap" style="width: 180px; height: 180px; margin: 0 auto; object-fit: contain; image-rendering: pixelated;" />
            </div>
            <figcaption class="mono" style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 11px; color: var(--ink-3);">Input: Jagged Upscaled Bitmap</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure style="margin: 0; text-align: center;">
            <div class="ws-checker" style="padding: 20px; border: 1px solid var(--line);">
              <img src="/assets/workshop/vector-after.svg" alt="Smooth local VTracer SVG path" style="width: 180px; height: 180px; margin: 0 auto; object-fit: contain;" />
            </div>
            <figcaption class="mono" style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 11px; color: var(--ink-3);">Output: Local VTracer .svg</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>2. Four-Step Prompt-to-SVG Workflow</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Pick a clean flat-color model</strong>: For icons and flat graphics, use <code>FLUX.2 Klein 4B</code> (Apache-2.0 commercial, ~10s in 4 steps) or <code>Qwen-Image 2.1</code> (when crisp embedded typography is needed). Prompt with <code>flat vector style, clean edges, solid color blocks</code> to minimize noisy gradients.</li>
        <li><strong>Remove background if needed</strong>: Run “Remove Background” on the stage to get a clean transparent PNG before entering the Workshop.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose Color or Binary mode</strong>: Open <strong>Workshop → Graphic &amp; Extract → Vectorize to SVG</strong>. Use <code>color</code> clustering for multi-tone badges and illustrations, or <code>binary</code> mode for monochrome line art, stamps, and logomarks with minimal anchor points.</li>
        <li><strong>Commit to your version tree &amp; drop into Figma</strong>: Saving stores the <code>.svg</code> linked to its parent image inside your local workspace—drag straight into Figma, Illustrator, or Inkscape to edit paths and fills.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>3. Best Candidates vs. Poor Fits</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap" style="margin: 22px 0 32px;">
        <table class="compare-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Asset Type</th>
              <th>Fit</th>
              <th>Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Monochrome Logo / Line Art / Stamp</strong></td>
              <td class="is-highlight">Excellent</td>
              <td>Binary mode produces minimal paths requiring almost no manual anchor cleanup.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Flat Illustration / Badge / UI Icon</strong></td>
              <td class="is-highlight">Good</td>
              <td>Solid color blocks recolor cleanly by layer; strip backgrounds first if complex.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Watercolor / Soft Gradient Art</strong></td>
              <td>Fair</td>
              <td>Gradients split into stepped bands; high-res PNG or upscaling works better.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Photorealistic Portraits &amp; Scenes</strong></td>
              <td>Not Recommended</td>
              <td>High-frequency textures yield massive fragmented SVGs that are hard to edit.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Want Claude Desktop or Cursor to generate project icons straight into your repo? Check out the <a href="/en/guides/mcp-claude-cursor-image" class="link-inline">MCP Integration Guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <GuideRelated currentSlug="ai-image-to-svg" lang="en" />
</BaseLayout>
